---
import { PRODUCT_NAME } from '../config';
import LegalPage from '../components/legal/LegalPage.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, SECURITY_SUBJECT } from '../components/legal/legal';

const reportHref = `mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=${encodeURIComponent(SECURITY_SUBJECT)}`;
---
<LegalPage
  title={`Security · ${PRODUCT_NAME}`}
  heading="Security"
  description={`How to report a security issue in ${PRODUCT_NAME}, and how the app protects your Mac and your repositories.`}
  ogTheme="dark"
>
  <div class="legal-card" id="report">
    <h2>Report a vulnerability</h2>
    <p>
      Email <a href={reportHref}><strong>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</strong></a> with the subject <strong>"{SECURITY_SUBJECT}"</strong>.
      Please don't open a public issue or post details anywhere until we've released a fix.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>What to include</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The {PRODUCT_NAME} version and macOS version.</li>
    <li>What an attacker can do, and the steps to reproduce it. A small test repository helps a lot.</li>
    <li>Whether the issue is already public, and how you'd like to be credited.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What happens next</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>We confirm we've received your report within 3 business days.</li>
    <li>We investigate, keep you updated, and agree a disclosure timeline with you. Our target is a fix within 90 days, sooner for serious issues.</li>
    <li>We ship the fix, note it in the <a href="/changelog">changelog</a>, and credit you if you want.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    We don't run a paid bug bounty. We won't take legal action against good-faith research that follows this page: test only on your own
    Macs and repositories, don't access other people's data, and don't attack our website, Polar or GitHub infrastructure.
  </p>

  <h3>In scope</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The {PRODUCT_NAME} app for macOS, including its handling of repositories, diffs, commit messages and branch names you didn't write.</li>
    <li>The licence check and the update mechanism.</li>
    <li>This website.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Bugs in <code>git</code> itself go to the Git project, and problems with checkout or payments go to Polar. If you're not sure, send it to us and we'll pass it on.
  </p>

  <h2 id="model">How {PRODUCT_NAME} is built to be safe</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your code stays on your Mac.</strong> {PRODUCT_NAME} has no account and no cloud sync. It works on your repositories through the <code>git</code> already on your Mac, with your existing Git configuration and credentials. It doesn't store or see your Git passwords or SSH keys.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sandboxed interface.</strong> The app's window runs in a sandboxed renderer with context isolation and no Node.js access. Only a small,
      fixed set of actions crosses into the part of the app that runs <code>git</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing loads from the internet into the window.</strong> A strict content security policy allows only the app's own scripts, styles and
      fonts. The window can't navigate away, and links open in your browser instead.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Repository content is treated as untrusted.</strong> Commit messages, file names, branch names and diffs are shown as text, never run as code.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Undo for every ref change.</strong> {PRODUCT_NAME} records each change it makes to branches, tags and HEAD, so a mistake, or a
      misbehaving operation, can be undone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Minimal network use.</strong> Besides the Git operations you start, the app talks only to Polar for licence checks and, once
      automatic updates ship, to our update server. The <a href="/privacy#app">privacy policy</a> lists exactly what each call sends.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Signed and notarized releases.</strong> Releases will be signed with an Apple Developer ID and notarized by Apple as soon as signing is
      available, so macOS can confirm the app comes from us and hasn't been altered. Until then, pre-release builds are unsigned and macOS asks
      you to confirm the first launch; see the <a href="/download">download page</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Payments never touch us.</strong> Polar, our merchant of record, handles checkout and card data. We never see or store card numbers.
    </li>
  </ul>
</LegalPage>
